\subsection{Scenario projections}\label{sec:r-homes}

Capacity projections overstate near-term housing by two orders of magnitude (Table~\ref{tab:homes}).
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Build-out.} In four cities, zoning and plan build-out exceed the homes added in the test window
@@bo_lo@@- to @@bo_hi@@-fold. Part of the gap is definitional, since build-out is an end state rather than a two-year
flow.
\item \textbf{Likelihood weighting} brings the totals to @@lk_lo@@--@@lk_hi@@ times the observed.
\item \textbf{Trend.} A five-year trend ranks districts better: Spearman @@trend_rho_lo@@ to @@trend_rho_hi@@, against
@@lk_rho_lo@@ to @@lk_rho_hi@@ for the likelihood-weighted projections, better in @@of_trend@@ cities.
\end{itemize}

The Manhattan rezonings show the same contrast for single decisions (Table~\ref{tab:replay},
Figure~\ref{fig:replay}). Capacity projected @@g_capacity@@ homes on development sites, or @@g_prorated@@ pro-rated to
2025. The likelihood-weighted projection gave @@g_likely@@, against @@g_observed@@ built, and the observed count lies
within its 95\% range in @@of_g@@ rezonings.

@@table_homes@@

